Let rpcbind run as an ordinary user.

Three things stop upstream rpcbind from starting without root, and no
capability can fix any of them:

  * it takes a lock on a fixed path under /var/run, a directory only root can
    write to;
  * it calls geteuid() and exits if the answer is not 0;
  * it then unconditionally drops privileges to the build-time RPC user, and
    setgroups(2) fails for a process that has no privileges to drop.

All three make sense for a system daemon started at boot.  Here rpcbind is not
one: it is started by hand out of a boot directory, gets port 111 from a single
CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE file capability, and exists to answer one 1980s
workstation.  So make the lock path settable at compile time (the build passes
-DRPCBINDDLOCK pointing into run/), drop the uid check, and only drop
privileges when there are some.  Behaviour when started as root is unchanged.

Nothing else is touched.  In particular the indirect-call forwarding the Sun-3
depends on is stock upstream behaviour, enabled with ./configure
--enable-rmtcalls.

--- a/src/rpcbind.c
+++ b/src/rpcbind.c
@@ -105,7 +105,9 @@
 /* who to suid to if -s is given */
 #define RUN_AS  "daemon"
 
+#ifndef RPCBINDDLOCK
 #define RPCBINDDLOCK "/var/run/rpcbind.lock"
+#endif
 
 int runasdaemon = 0;
 int insecure = 0;
@@ -170,10 +172,12 @@
 		setrlimit(RLIMIT_NOFILE, &rl);
 	}
 
-	if (geteuid()) { /* This command allowed only to root */
-		fprintf(stderr, "Sorry. You are not superuser\n");
-		exit(1);
-	}
+	/*
+	 * Upstream refuses to start unless euid is 0.  Here rpcbind is not a
+	 * system daemon: it is started by hand from a boot directory and gets
+	 * port 111 from a single file capability, so the check would only
+	 * prevent it from running at all.
+	 */
 
 	/*
 	 * Make sure we use the local service file 
@@ -248,7 +252,7 @@
         	err(1, "fork failed");
 	}
 
-	if (runasdaemon || rpcbinduser) {
+	if (geteuid() == 0 && (runasdaemon || rpcbinduser)) {
 		struct passwd *p;
 		char *id = runasdaemon ? RUN_AS : rpcbinduser;
 
@@ -272,8 +276,13 @@
                         exit(1);
                 }
 		if (setgroups(0, NULL) == -1) {
+			/*
+			 * Not fatal here.  A user namespace can present an
+			 * euid of 0 without granting the privilege to call
+			 * setgroups(2); as real root this call cannot fail,
